I'm not sure I really understand what you're trying to do. If you want to
upgrade systems that have an older version of Tru64 UNIX to v4.0F, then just
do an installupdate on each system using the v4.0F OS CD. If you already
have one system running and want to "clone" it's boot disk onto several
other systems that have no OS installed, there's several ways to do that.

One would be to install a second drive in the running system, disklabel it
with identical sized partitions as your working boot disk, then copy the
root, usr, and var (if not part of usr) partitions from the existing disk to
the new disk (you could use vdump and pipe the output to vrestore, see the
man page for vdump for an example). Of course you'd have to modify some
files like /etc/fstab to fix up SCSI device numbering, etc. Then you could
move the disk to your new system, boot the genvmunix kernel, run sizer and
merge the kernel config files (to account for hardware differences between
the two systems), build a new kernel and boot it up. I know this method will
work because I've done it before between two completely different
AlphaServers (cloned a boot disk from a 2100 and used it in a 4100).

Anyway, if you could give me more specific details on what you're trying to
do I could probably answer the question better.

--> Jay


