My environment is based on RIS, with clients installing from the network.

I went thru this last summer, and after trying to work from the 
documentation, and finding out that the system was left unconfigured, 
made some scripts that will fully install a new machine, patch and 
configure it.

Basically, what they do is hook into the boot sequence just before going 
to multi-user and there run all installation and configuration, with the 
required reboots and all. We can even configure DCE and DFS this way.

The scripts make the disk partitions based on some rules of thumb, installs
required software, which can be different between machines and makes life 
much happier for the sysadmin.

When a new machine arrives, all I have to do is note the name, IP and MAC 
numbers into the bootp and ris databases, and on the client give the 
command to boot  from the network, and return one hour later to a fresh 
machine that is ready for the users to log onto.

I have also modified one of the RIS program in order to reduce the load of 
RIS server with 99 percent, so the time to install a client is not dependent
on how many clients that are installing on the same time. The server easily
handles 40 simultane installing clients with a load average of 0.2

If anyone wants this script, just mail, and I will put together a kit.

