The DB15 is an AUI Ethernet attachment.  Do not even attempt to connect a
monitor to it.

Compaq sells an adapter for about $70 U.S. that will allow a new multisync
PC monitor to work with that system.  I have posted the part number on
comp.os.vms in the past, and would need to seach on http://www.deja.com to
look it up.  The adapter will only work on monitors that meet the required
specifications though, so older multisync monitors may not work.

If you have one of the older models under service contract, and it dies so
that field service replaces it with a new one, that is the adapter that they
bring out.

As I do not know what the electronics inside it are, I do not know what
third party RGB to SVGA adaptors can be used.

> One word of caution:  If you are running VMS, leave a keyboard connected
> to the VS3100.  You will still use the keyboard on the terminal and not
> the one on the VS3100.
>
> If a keyboard is not connected, VMS wants a VAXserver license.  If the
> keyboard is connected, VMS uses a VAXstation license.

Under the OpenVMS hobby license program, both license keys are the same
price $0.00 U.S.
see http://www.montagar.com/

Under OpenVMS 5.5-2 and later, the VAX 3100 license key will work with
either configuration, the VAXstation one may only work if you have the
keyboard connected.

