Thomas,

On my multia, the cpu was running too hot to touch. However, if you look at the
fan, you see that it has a heat sense thermistor which is tywrapped to the end
of the psu case. This speeds up the fan as the temp rises, but you can fool it
into running at maximum speed all the time by relocating the sense thermistor
between the cpu heat sink fins + use a bit of epoxy to hold in place. This alone
results in a few degrees lower cpu temperature, and runs the cpu quite cool if
you also use a bit of card to form a funnel round the cpu heat sink. 

Still wasn't satisfied - have now cut out the metal grill in the side of the
case (the perforated metal restricts the airflow probably by 50% of fan
throughput) and replaced the fan with a fixed speed high flow model which keeps
the whole box within reasonable limits, including the cpu heat sink.

I would say you are pushing your luck if you are overclocking without arranging
for more airflow. Neat little box, but needed a _lot_ more sorting out in the
thermal management dept which was marginal even with the original cpu...

Rgrds, Chris





