In article <0f7f3aa0.c167aaf8@usw-ex0107-042.remarq.com>, Chris Doran <chrisj.doranNOchSPAM@physics.org.invalid> wrote:
..
>> The DHV11 is a Quad board. I'm fairly sure it can live in a Q-Q
>> slot or a Q-CD
>> slot without too much difficulty.
>
>True, but according to the manual p 2-6, you _must_ set jumpers W1 and
>W2 correctly: both in for Q/Q, both out for Q/CD.

That will be because in a Q-Q slot the board needs to ensure that grant is 
passed along to the next board (which, in a serpentine Q-Q arrangement like 
the BA23 and BA123, logcally follows the "CD" fingers on the DHV). So in a Q-Q 
slot the DHV11 looks like two dual width cards so far as the grant signals are 
concerned. In a Q-CD slot the jumpers are set to pass nothing along the CD 
slots in case it interferes with the next card.

I recall that the KDA plays the same tricks, except that with the KDA you need 
to physically cut a zero ohm resistor when it lives in a Q-CD slot. I decided 
to cut nothing since I have nothing in the following CD slot and it all works 
fine.

Antonio

