It's either 19200 or 9600, 8, None. No need to try all those other 
combinations.

>settings.  I tried with both a straight wired and a null modem cable.
>Serial terminal was a Laptop with hardware flow control disabled.
>Program was QModem for DOS, running in Hex-Debug mode (so i could see

The port on the back of the VS3100 is MMJ (the one with the printer icon). If 
you are going to a VT300 terminal (or later) which also has an MMJ connector 
the only variable is the cable (which could either be straight through or 
flipped - 99% are the "correct one", I just cannot remember which that is 
right now).

If you are going to a PC serial port you need the appropriate DB25-MMJ or 
DB9-MMJ adapter. Beware: DEC used DB9 early on and then IBM used different 
pinouts so if you have homebrewed something you have to be careful of this.

I no longer have the fight adapter kicking around so I cannot look up the part 
number for you. If you have a VT300 (or even VT220) kicking around I would 
suggest using that to get one variable out of the way. S3 up is the alternate 
console mode.

I assume that there is definitely power getting to the machine and the LEDs at 
the back light up?

>every byte coming through).  Just a bunch of 0x00's, and a single
>0x80.  Tried also with the little switch in both positions. Also tried
>with the keyboard plugged and unplugged.  Also tried both serial
>ports.  Nothing.  Unless the adapter I have is bad...  Anyone know
>which RJ-11 pin to what DB-25f pin this should be wired to?

