The first PDP-11 I worked on was ordered well in advance of the
production of the machine. Among the material provided to keep the
sale going was a description of the machine's architecture, which I
inherited.

It was, in fact, a description of quite a different instruction set.
Those familiar with Bell's book on Computer Architecture may recall
him saying that they prototyped a number of designs (using a DEC-10
simulator and his ISP (Instruction Set Processor) language I guess).

My guess is that the documentation we were given was of an attempt to
build an optimised Fortran machine.

Well, I'm ashamed to say that I can't find the document now. The last
time I looked at ancient document was a decade ago. The ink on the
photocopy had almost completely faded at that time.


But to come back to the point you raise, I guess the only way to
secure these documents is to scan them in and make them broadly
available over the Web or on DVD.

Museums are often just a different way of burying and losing stuff. 


Ian




