Actually, they are called dual width. This is really getting into DEC
folklore.
However, the original flip-chips had one "tounge", and were only about
half
the height of the cards you see in a Q-bus. And they are the norm.
So a card with two connectors is dual-width, four is quad (the full
width
of a Q-bus backplane), sex tounges will be a hex-width card. Then you
have
double height as well. Many q-bus cards are thus dual width and double
height. Some are quad width. Many Unibus cards are hex width, with some
quad width cards too. For single height you have to go back a bit more
in time. I have a pdp-8/i close by, which are build purely by single
height
cards. Most of them also single width, but a few are double width cards.
 
> Depending on where on the Qbus you need to grant the continuity, you may
