In article <38914B59.696EC820@vl.videotron.ca>, JF Mezei <jfmezei.spamnot@vl.videotron.ca> wrote:
>Q bus grant continuity,  card number M9047 only fills one side of the QBUS. So
>I guess that is called half width.

That's a dual width card - it has two "tongues" or connectors. The Qbus is 
dual width. In the older boxes (BA23, BA123) the bus starts out as Q-CD and 
then serpentines Q-Q. 

The VAX 4000-300 has Q-CD all the way down. This means that (lookign from the 
front and with the box the correct way up!) the Q-bus runs (from right to 
left?) along the top two slots and the bottom two slots are CD (i.e. private 
interconnect between boards).

It might help if you post exactly what lives where in your VAX 4000-300.

>
>DHV11 RS232 controllers took up full width, but the DHQ11 took the same half
>width as a grant continuity.
>

The DHV11 is a Quad board. I'm fairly sure it can live in a Q-Q slot or a Q-CD 
slot without too much difficulty. The DHQ11 is a Dual height card and must 
live in a Qbus slot - putting it in the lower half of anywhere in a VAX 
4000-300 won't get you very far.

>Depending on where on the Qbus you need to grant the continuity, you may
>require one or two cards next to each other. (On the Microvax II, there is a
>small diagram which descriped the serpentine flow of cards.


On the BA123 (World box, has coasters to let you push it around, makes a nice 
seat!) there are markings to show you where Q-CD ends and Q-Q starts. On the 
BA23 there are no markings: even if there were they would soon become obscured 
by the torn skin and flecks of blood that one is required to deposit therein 
whenever modifying the configuration.

Now there DID used to be a single height grant card for Qbus but it had 
certain disadvantages: it was much easier to insert incorrectly in the wrong 
slot of the two you had to pick (and it was easier to put in upside down too), 
and since it was not full length (imagine an M9047 sliced down the middle and 
then sliced a few inches from the "tongue" to make it short) it was much 
trickier to insert at all (entire limbs were required as offerings to the 
votive bus, mere skin scrapings would have been a joy!).

I don't suppose this helps much with your RXV21problem.

I have it listed as not Q22 compatible but I don't know where I got that from 
or what it means. I suspect that maybe it can only do Q18 DMA. This shouldn't 
stop you seeing it since all SYSGEN (and the console) do usually is poke 
around in the right place looking for a CSR or two and those should still be 
visible. (And the vector setting won't matter until you actually try to do 
some I/O).


