In article <388D5161.221316B3@micronas.com>,
  Paul Blenderman <Paul.Blenderman@micronas.com> wrote:
> Ever thought of using NT? Our NT systems have an uptime comparable
> to our VMS and Unix systems. The best of both worlds.

As others in this group can testify this is very unusual. For example
we have not had a single VMS system crash or needed to reboot to fix a
VMS problem in the last year. Our HP Unix servers are rebooted once a
week (although this may be mainly for historical reasons) and heavily
loaded NT servers will usually hang up or crash  anytime between a week
and a couple of months if not rebooted - usually with some sort of out
of resources error and probably due to memory leaks or system resources
not being freed. Advice from all sorts of Microsoft 'experts'
is "expected behaviour - reboot once a week". The same advice is often
given by Unix specialists but you will not find any VMS experts handing
out this advice except possibly in very obscure situations as a work
around.

A university admin network I once administered ran for five years
serving hundreds of PCs with file, mail, print etc services without a
single crash or unplanned reboot (Pathworks 4). We would have had
similar figures for the student servers had they not been upgraded to
Pathworks 5 but during their last year running Pathworks 4 I cannot
recall a crash either. Pathworks 5 crashed the university's VMS systems
several times a week (a day even some times) for months and helped
bring about the demise of VMS for student use there until threats of
legal action finally woke up DEC and caused them to devote enough
resources to our problems. In the end we succeeded in bringing the
system's stability back under control (at worst needed a Pathworks
restart from time to time - not a system crash or reboot).

Bottom line: If your NT and Unix systems stay up as long as the VMS
systems because the VMS systems occasionally crash then you have a VMS
problem which needs looked at. Nobody should be experiencing VMS system
crashes except on the rarest of occasions and/or with new releases and
if you find one you should expect a fix to be made available - ie a
coding bug can be found rather than just sloppy coding throughout. Yes
even DEC sometimes screw up but they are still capable of fixing the
problem.

Microsoft claim to have put a lot of work into Windows 2000 to nail
down these server problems but it remains to be seen how well they have
done. On the desktop Windows 2000 does appear to be remarkably robust
for a Microsoft product and the few desktop crash problems we found
during testing of the late betas and release candidates seem to have
been fixed in the final release. In fact I'm the first to be critical
of Microsoft when they deserve it but NT 2000, at least on the desktop
and used as a better NT 4, has proved good enough to start rolling out
to users immediately. Just how well it performs when loaded as a server
remains to be seen as we're only just about to start extensive tests in
this configuartion. The current release notes lead me to expect this is
where we will start to see problems.

As always YMMV.

--
Alan Greig


