In article <388DE8BA.8D5D5B41@nortelnetworks.com>,
Rob Hounsell  <hounsell@nortelnetworks.com> wrote:
>Hi,
>
>  Anyone know of a good procedure for cloning disk images on Alphas?
>Ideally, we'd like to use a master CD image to create corresponding disk
>images for upgrading systems. We thought about using dd, but I'm
>wondering about cloning to different disk sizes (and thus possibly
>wasting disk space). Conversely, I don't know the exhaustive list of
>files required to create a successful clone if we went the filesystem
>route i.e. copying files/directories.

DU/Tru64 has the ability to make clones of systems.  It's documented in
the "Sharing Software on a Local Area Network" book on the documentation
CD.

Basically what you do is install a system, setup a RIS server, and
move the /var/adm/smlogs/install.cdf file over to the RIS server.
You may have to tweak the file some for different disk types, but
it's not that big a deal.  Configure the RIS server to install the
target system, and boot the target from it's ethernet adapter.  If
you've setup everything correctly, it will do an install and you end
up with a system setup like the first one, minus things like setting
up NIS, /etc/resolv.conf, etc.  The cloning process has a way to run
a script after the install is done so you can setup those things.


