tar and cp both have the problem that they will copy hardlinks as files,
tar also looses setuid bits which is a major pain for this kind of thing.

In the past I have had good success with the procedure of using a custom
boot cd (see the back of the install guide I think although it may have
moved look for the section on advanced installs). The process is useful
since you can set it up to prompt for the info that varies from machine
to machine (hostname etc) and still keep the rest of the stuff the same.

One good tip if you do this is to then make use of the floppy drive record
all of the information you provide at install time, keep the files on it
uptodate and keep a unique floppy for each machine (use cron,store it in
the drive, it's not critical only useful)

The other partitions besides / and /usr, can then be cloned as compressed
vdump savesets, and a script written to create the partitions and extract
the files. Use default partition sizes read from the install floppy which
you can override, ask for the sizes before you start the install, and time
out if no response is given in a reasonable time, this way you can do
automated or partially automated restores.

Then if you ever need to rebuild a replacement for that workstation, you
use the standard boot cd plus the files on the floppy to automate the recovery.
When the amount of difference between the bootcd and the desired workstation
is too large to fit on a floppy, then it's time for a new bootcd.

Hope this helps

Adam

