Johnny Billquist  <Johnny.Billquist@netinsight.se> wrote:
>emanuel stiebler wrote:
>> http://minnie.cs.adfa.edu.au/PUPS/Images/ken-and-den.jpg
>> both are standing in front of a 11/20, and the year is about 1970 ;-)
>
>Ah, but the 11/20 says "11/20" on the front, so it was definitely the
>not the earliest time. At the start the pdp-11/20 was labelled just
>"pdp-11" on the front, I believe.

Indeed.  I think the naked "pdp11" (no hyphen (or uppercase) until the
Micro PDP-11s came out) labelling lasted until the new models (/45, /10,
/05, others?) came out in '72, but it might have been before that.

>Also, just because DMR says so, it don't mean that he can't be off by
>a year or so. I mean, it's not like it was yesterday or so...

DMR doesn't say so:

	http://cm.bell-labs.com/cm/cs/who/dmr/picture.html

says:

	Here's a publicity photo from about 1972, showing Ken and me in
	front of a PDP-11.

This photo got discussed at length (with the DMR contributing) in this
newsgroup a while ago.  Someone spotted that there's the front panel of
a pdp11/45 peeking out from behind the rightmost ASR33 (and just to the
left of the labelled 11/20), which dates the photo to no earlier than
'72.  The /45 had a proper virtual memory system (unlike the /20 and the
PDP-7), and was therefore able to support proper multiprocessing system.

-- don



