Bear in mind that because of cross-posting I'm reading this in
<news:comp.text.interleaf>. And most Digital environments are usually
heterogeneous and also include excellent communications. And of course it's
pretty easy to email a document or file from one environment to another, so
the fact that DECwrite is Digital-specific and some converter products run
in other environments shouldn't be an issue...

Mike wasn't specific about what output formats he *could* write, hence my
mention of DX and WPL which are Digital specific [if elderly].

In email I have also made suggestions about further multiformat document
converters:
    <http://www.keypak.com/product.htm>
    <http://www.blueberry.com/>
and suggestions about SGML output.

A further suggestion I made is that if Mike can write PostScript out of
DECwrite he should be able to strip out the text or convert to PDF using
GSview + Ghostscript. Get it from:
    <http://www.cs.wisc.edu/~ghost/index.html>. It's free.

And once you've got a document into PDF, you can view it using Acrobat
Reader and copy text and graphics readily into other applications. So here's
a suggested answer to Mike's problem, leaving the output in [shock horror!]
MS-Word, which is what he asked for:

1. 'Print' out of DECwrite to a PostScript file
2. Transfer the output to a PC by whatever means available
3. View the PostScript with GSview
4. 'Print' the document to a .pdf file from GSview
5. View the document with Acrobat Reader
6. Copy text and images to the clipboard
7. Paste into an MS-Word document

Yes, there are a number of steps, but I'm pretty confident it will work. And
the tools used [GSview+Ghostscript, Acrobat Reader] are free and readily
available, which is always a bonus!

--
Regards

Richard Perkin
