In article <388FF7F6.8995B822@bell-labs.com>,
Dennis Ritchie  <dmr@bell-labs.com> wrote:
>Since I have the manual in hand,
>I wonder whether the Computer History museum or Ebay is the
>best destination when it comes time to dispose of my assets.

Old iron hobbyists always seem to groan whenever someone puts something rare
like this on eBay, but I don't get it -- "expensive" beats "unavailable"
any day.  At least with eBay, you can get whatever relics you need to get
your machine working, as long as you're willing to go without food to pay
for it all!  So at least it's a choice.  When stuff is just put up for grabs
it's usually first come first serve so it always goes to someone who has time
to read news 100 times per day and/or has the best newsfeed, not necessarily
to the person who needs it most or can return the favor the best.

A good museum which makes its archives available to the public would obviously
be the nicest place to donate really rare magical stuff, as long as you can
count on them to keep going.  It's heartbreaking how often you hear about
people or organizations that amassed a bunch of really priceless stuff and
then vanished.  Or sometimes they develop an attitude and won't let anyone
see stuff which was donated for the purpose of being publicly available.
That happens at any kind of museum though I guess...

John Wilson
D Bit



