Hello all fellow hardware hackers!

I am the proud new owner of a VAX 6510. I would like to run this on
single phase power. Although I can see how this can be done on older
systems, the 6510 seems to have a different power supply system.  I
have 220VAC at 100 amps. More than enough, but single phase.. I'm
basicaly looking for some details on the power supply, and opinions
concerning the same.

This question may need the answers of a hardware guru who actually
knows the guts of the 6510's power system. I have the cojones to
modify it, but I'd like a few opinions from some experienced 65xx
power supply folks before I do. Don't want to blow the thing up! I do
want to license it for hobbyist use. (Yes I am insane but I have alot
of electricity)

Please correct me if I am wrong, but upon examination of the insides
of the 6510 cabinet (H9759-CA), I think I am looking at:

A box with power cord and contactor (0H405-E), which feeds 3 phase
(208V-Y) power to:

A box (the H7206B, which is also the power logic control for the
cabiet power system) with a 3 phase full wave rectifier [and maybe a
switchmode power factor corrector, but I have not opened it] which
puts out 300VDC to:

The H7242, H7214, and H7215 switching power supplies (regulators)
which actually convert this high voltage down to the CPU and bus
operating voltages.

If this is so, then should I not reengineer the H7206 slightly so that
it can supply ripple-free 300VDC from a single phase fullwave bridge?
Aside from inrush considerations, the simple solution would be to
triple the value of the main filter capacitor since the ripple
frequency would only be 120Hz instead of 360Hz. Other considerations
would of course be the low voltages logic system inside the H7206, but
that is probably minor.

If possible, please address comments also to:
vaxhackery@worldnet.att.net

Thanks!





