In article <86acfg$g5g$1@nnrp1.deja.com>, david_m_curry@my-deja.com writes:
:I am considering getting a VAXserver 3300 to ad to my hobbyist cluster
:at home, but there is no information on the net that I can find so that
:I can see if it is suitable for home use.  Can anybody tell me what bus
:it has? DSSI is my guess, but I don't know.  How many VUPS?  How big is
:it?  Can it run on house current (120 VAC)?  Will my electric bill go
:up noticably?  Is it reliable?  Etc.

  The MicroVAX 3300, VAXserver 3300, MicroVAX 3400, and VAXserver 3400
  are all the same basic system.  The "3300" had fewer Q-bus slots and
  storage bays (former using the BA215, vs the bigger BA213 used on the
  "3400"), but the electronics are otherwise similar -- all use the same
  KA640 processor, and all with Q-bus and integrated DSSI bus support...

  The "ALPHA5. Seeking performance information for Alpha (and VAX) systems?"
  section of the FAQ has some pointers that will be of interest.  You'll be 
  able to find your way to various spots from there, including:

    http://www.digital.com/alphaserver/archive/vax/mv3300.html

  The enclosure is the BA215 6-slot, a half-width version of the rather more
  common BA213 12-slot Q-bus enclosure...

  BA215 dimensions are 13.6"W x 27"H x 17.8"D.  Bays for two DSSI disks and 
  one tape.  ("3400" series had three DSSI disk bays, and one tape.  Wider 
  box...)

  I don't have the power requirements handy, but I don't recall it as being
  particularly remarkable for systems of that vintage -- 120V...

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




