Er, not sure how familiar you are with the 6000 series, but a 6000-440 with
4 cpus and 256mb of ram
is only a measured 550w of actual consumption. ie 2.3 amps @ 240vac.
When it was still a mere 6000-310 with 64mb it drew a measured 1.8 amps
at 240vac single phase, or a miserable 432 watts, from a domestic
(Australian) wall outlet)

The machine might be big and heavy, (384kg or around 845lbs) but a power
sucker it's not.
As big vaxen go, this is one of the best to run at home, it's all surface
mount technology,
and is quite easy to get going on single phase.

Disk drives are a different matter, but it's possible to have low
consumption stuff that will
speak to it.  Depends on the adapter fit, and what drives came with the
unit.
A RA90 1gb SDI drive is around 150w once spun up.  So a couple of those with
a
KDB50 in the Vax wouldn't increase the air temp too much.
If you have a scsi controller it gets even better.

> . . . I haven't taken any measurements per-se but my guess it that the dec
> 3000 that I have running back here in the spare bedroom results in a 1.5
to
> 3 degree increase in the ambient temperature.

Not familiar with that model.  Desktop MIPS box?
At 110v, how many amps is it drawing? (actual - not what the data plate
says -
the 6000 data plate say 4.7KVA, which is the max it can handle, but it USES
a LOT less than that, unless you
have a truly ENORMOUS card fit.)

