Ah, an interesting problem.

First,
On power up, the keyboard configures itself as a simple PC style keyboard.
If it is hooked up to a computer, say the Alpha, and the Alpha is set to
speak with the SRM, then the SRM console will do a query on the keyboard and
attempt to get the keyboard to speak to the SRM in some fashion it
understands.

If the keyboard were not hooked up to the Alpha and the keyboard port were
left dangling with nothing there, then the SRM will not properly configure
anything and what shows up in OpenVMS, or Tru64 UNIX land is anybodies
guess.

The technique is to purchase a special Guardian, made exclsuively for
Compaq, to talk specifically with the SRM at power on, keyboard or not and
help the SRM and thus the OS properly configure themselves to work with a
keyboard. WIth one of these, you'll end up with only a few off or weird
characters from a PC style keyboard.

These special Guardians are sold by CSS and made for us by Raritan. They are
not the same as the PC guardian, they have special mapping code as the LK
series keyboards are not PC keyboard and prodcue special codes.

rtt





