I didn't pay attention to the site address, but sometimes people don't
realize just how much faster a little Alphaserver is. It was hard for us
to imagine that a little 2'x2'x3' system cabinet and a few BA350s would
replace a whole room full of big Vaxes and RA72 disks. I had to
guestimate the VUP rating of the 3800 and take a huge leap of faith that
Alphas were as good as DEC wanted us to believe.

Also the I/O from direct PMAZ-C scsi controllers to RZ28/RZ29's was
significantly faster than any CI device could ever hope. Of course, you
lose the cluster failover capability of HSC/HSZ/HSJ controllers, but we
ran that 3800 for 4 years with maybe 10 hrs of downtime to add more
memory and disk controllers.

Funny thing is, we replaced the big VAXes about 2 months before our
company relocated to a new building. The 8820/6420 were left in the old
computer room to return to a leasing company. We got everything moved
into the new computer room and one of the Executives came to the new
site and asked where all the computers were and why we weren't up and
running at the new site. The operations manager smiled and pointed to
the one small rack mount system and said "that's all we've had for
months and everything's running perfectly".

We'd unplugged the old machines 2 months before and no-one knew we'd
switched to Alpha other than the login banner and the incredible speed.

Our applications were easy to port, COBOL, FMS, Datatrieve, Corvision
and DEC Basic. We VESTed anything that didn't have source code.

Sure hope the original poster's 8800 applications aren't using packed
decimal data. 6000 series machines can be 50 times slower.

			*JM*

