Hi there,

I've tested it on the cluster and it works (I'm the one who works at
the moment on the software mentioned in the first posting).
The mapping is done by mmap() on a file created with creat().
msync() is called with MS_INVALIDATE | MS_SYNC.

But under some strange circumstances it seems that updates aren't seen
by a reader on the other node of the cluster (as you said: it may be
not 'guaranteed').

In addition we have an performance problem if the changes on the mapped
file are distributed randomly:
VMS seem to make no difference between the Writer (who called msync())
(or any other process on the same node which has mapped the file) and
the Reader on an other node. So if the former Writer reads its own data
a file access is done (but of course it is not necessary).
If I'm not wrong, in a one node system msync() is not necessary at all,
to see changes from one process made by an other to the shared memeory
(mapped file) ? Am I ?

Has anyone any ideas/hints on performance relevant arguments of the
creat()-function or anything else ?
And is there a chance that the data is on disk after the msync() call
has returned or a way to 'guarantee' this.

Thanks in advance,

Martin Schmidt


