In article <387EB62E.C0AEEDC9@llamacom.com>, Ben Sinclair <ben@llamacom.com> wrote:

>I have a VAXStation 3200 that I'm trying to get running again, with
>NetBSD if I can. I know nothing about it and have so far been unable to
>find good documentation to help me figure out what's wrong with it. It
>had two hard drives, but doesn't boot. I'm not sure that they even work.
>As far as I can tell, the only boot device it knows about is the network
>interface.

[snip]

>No default boot device has been specified.
>
>Available devices.
>-XQA0 (08-00-2B-09-40-9B)
>
>Device? [XQA0]: 
>
>(BOOT/R5:0 XQA0)
>

Either it really has no other device or the battery is low and has
failed to retain a previous setting.

Try SHOW DEVICE to see what is really there.

>>>>show qbus
>Scan of Qbus I/O Space
>-20001468 (772150) = FFFF (154) RQDX3/KDA50/RRD50/RQC25/KFQSA-DISK

>>>>show RLV12
>>>>show UQSSP
>UQSSP Disk Controller 0 (772150)
>-?

Looks like you have a controller of some sort, probably an RQDX3 (since that 
is what would have shipped in a VAXstation 3200). That should have an RD53 or
(hopefully) RD54 attached. If it doesn't show up under SHOW DEVICE then it not 
there, dead or not properly connected.

If there is a disk actually in the box check the cabling all the way from the 
RQDX3 (module M7555) right up to the back of the disk (two cables here, plus 
power).

Once you have that sorted you should be able to do
        BOOT DUA0 (or whatever it happens to be set to)
and (after replacing the battery pack if necessary) you can
do
        SET BOOT DUA0
so it remembers it for next time.

Antonio

