Compaq made it clear that they think that customers would be confused by
having NT run on 2 64 bit architectures and that ISVM's are not interested in
supporting 2 (Alpha and IA64).

Seems pretty clear to me that Compaq has no reason to revive NT on Alpha.
Intel will eventually have a 64 bit chip which performs, well, eeee,
acceptably. And all the sheep of the world will flock to it. And Compaq will
have products to serve those sheep.

But Compaq knows that Alpha based solutions will devliver a consistently
higher performance than whatever Intel can deliver, especially with upcoming
switch based machines. So it is to Compaq's advantage to prevent NT to run on
Alpha since NT will be relegated to the poor performing Intel chips while
Compaq products will deliver much higher performance.

If Linux takes off, then this policy will be a winner for Compaq since it will
be able to sell Alpha based Linux boxes that outperform that Windows stuff
running in IA64 and 8086s.



