Jennifer,

I gather by your message, that you have not done anything knowingly to
change the system-level software.  Sounds like you may well be another
victim of that world-class productivity(?) tool, Windows 95.

As a stopgap measure until you can figure out what else is wrong, add
a directive to your CONFIG.SYS to access the CD-ROM drive in DOS mode.
Then add a matching directive for MSCDEX.EXE in the system
AUTOEXEC.BAT file.

If the above does not work, and the CD-ROM drive is still
inaccessable, chalk it up to a CD-ROM drive that croaked and needs to
be replaced.  DEC used decent quality CD-ROM drives made by Toshiba,
for the most part.  But quality these days ain't what it used to be,
even with leading name brand suppliers... Ben Myers

