> I've being doing some thinking about the drop in sales for Alpha/NT
> prior to THE END. Just before the introduction of the EV6 based systems
> there was little performance difference between the fastest Pentiums
> and the fastest EV5 machines. Our benchmarks suggested that we could
> get perhaps a 1.5 times performance improvement in finite element
> analysis times for a 4 to 1 cost penalty. There was no clear business
> case favouring the Alpha. With the XP1000 we found that we were getting
> performance boosts of between 4 and 6 to one over the Pentium II. I
> guess this is because the EV6 virtually never stalls with the
> particular instruction mix while the EV5 did. We bought two XP1000s to
> run a particular application on NT (not available under VMS any more)
> based on our experience with an evaluation model. Also we looked at
> running Unigraphics on XP1000s (potential 80 units this plant alone)
> instead of on HP workstations. EV4/5 based systems again were getting a
> bit long in the tooth but the XP1000 established a clear advantage
> again. Fortunately before we could go down that line Microsoft/Compaq
> dropped Alpha/NT and EDS dropped Unigraphics on *both* Alpha/NT and Tru-
> 64. Both companies used sales figures for an outdated Alpha processor
> to justify dropping support on the new models.
>
> If our situation was typical then it would be interesting to know the
> sales percentages for Alpha/NT on the XP1000 as opposed to those for
> the last year on EV5 workstations. To sum up we didn't buy Alpha/NT
> because it was not economical to do so and there was no clear
> advantage. As soon as that situation changed we started buying them and
> almost immediately the rug was pulled - risking pulling the plug on our
> VMS ES40s in the fallout. Timing is everything...
> --
> Alan Greig
