Both the 8000 and the 6000 series use three phase power connectors.

Now in Europe that generally means a red CEE form connector.
These things come in different sizes, 16A 25A 32A and upwards.
The three phases run 120 degrees out of phase (for voltage).
Each phase towards neutral gives you the "normal" voltage used by
hifi sets, kitchen equipment etc.
Real <g> computers are power hungry and since power=voltage*current
its better to increase voltage for high power devices in order to reduce
current
and hence losses in transformes, copper wires etc.

Since Europe (the UK excepted) runs at 230 Volt single phase, which allows
you to come up with 380Volts between a correct combination of two adjacent
phases
produces 380Volts.

The use runs at 110 V and that would result in around 200V between phases I
guess.

The 6000's require less power, but you may put one RA8x disk or two RA9x
disks
inside the cpu cabinet. That'd make close to an 8800.

Hans Vlems


