:Me neither, but that's the model type written on the DEC Service card.

  Post the model number from the sticker with the serial number...
  There were a couple of systems known as the VS210-A2 and VS210-A3...
  These are VAXstation II systems with the VCB01 (QVSS) graphics...
  The VS210 used the BA23 9-slot enclosure...

:The guy who gave it to me said it had been expanded to be similar to a
:VAX 3900 as far as I remember.

  Somebody probably yanked out the KA630 CPU (and any associated memory) 
  and replaced it with a KA655 (and associated memory).  Or you have
  something completely different...

  If you look at the CPU module, you'll probably see the number M7625
  stamped on the edge...  MS650 memory code is M7621 or M7622, depending
  on the particular module -- with the KA655, you will/should probably
  see only M7622.

:I don't remember the numbers on the QBUS cards...

  We need that...

:> > I managed to get it running and booting VMS a couple of times, but now
:> > it's sort of dead.
:> > When I turn it on it starts to change numbers on the LED display, and
:> > after a while it stops at 3 and stays there.
:> > Nothing is displayed on the screen, allthough I've gotten som garbage a
:> > couple of times.
:> 
:> What kind of a hard drive do you have in the system?  Off the top of my head
:> I'd guess you've got a dead Hard Drive, probably an RD53.  Yes, that guess
:> is based on personal experience :^(
:
:There's a RA70 and a RA71. I have four of them, but they're sligthly
:different. Two of them look as they're a new type with RA70 written with
:big letters on the front.

  If you have RA disks, you have (need) a KDA50 disk controller...
  (The KDA50 has two Q-bus modules, and four fat (usually) rectangular
  cables (usually black) attached with rectangular-ended connectors and 
  a pair of screws.)  There are rather similar disks -- the RF series 
  such as the RF71 -- that utilized the KFQSA DSSI disk controller, and
  an HDA very similar to the RA7x series...

:...but I've actually managed to get it in the air again. The machine was
:actually OK. My VT terminal just didn't like this machine.
:I replaced the VT510 with a VT220 and then everything worked and I can
:now boot VMS 5.5 :)

  The terminal works fine, the usual problem involves the settings...

:> > The cable and MMJ connectors are OK and the VT console is at 9600 baud.
:> 
:> MMJ connectors?  Now I'm really wondering what you've got.  Is the console
:> cable plugged into a 9-pin socket, or a MMJ socket?
:
:A MMJ sitting on a small print connected to a QBUS card with two small
:cables.
:The cover is removed on the QBUS side due to cables on the hard drive
:controller (they're too big to allow the side cover to be on).

  Running with the covers off?  (Your neighbors might complain about the
  interference, and you might run into some overheating problems depending 
  on the airflow through the box...)

:> > BTW: Does this model require 3 phase 220 Volts or just two ? (I think my
:> > problems can be related to this, allthough I'm fairly sure I've used the
:> > same cable and 220 Volt plug now and when it worked).
:> 
:> 220V....  Um, what country are you in?  
:
:Denmark. We use 220 Volts here.
:
:> Any Q-Bus based system I've seen
:> runs off standard 120V AC.  What kind of a chassis is the computer in?
:
:A fairly large one. About 1 meter high on wheels and half a meter wide
:and deep. Room for 3 RA70's in front and one TKZ50.

  That box was _never_ a VS210...  That sounds like a BA200 or BA400
  series enclosure -- one of the s-box variants...

  The BA213 has 12 slots, the BA215 has 6 -- given the number of disks
  and the tape, you likely have the BA213.  (There are a couple of BA400 
  series boxes, I don't remember the Q-bus slot counts off-hand...)

:There's an open hole in the upper left side I don't know what's for.
:Seems to be for some panel.

  The one thing I've seen mounted in this area was the bulkhead for
  the KDA50.  Given comments elsewhere, this is probably why you
  have the covers off...

:It's a little bigger than a normal 3900, and generally looks (alot)
:older than a 3900.

  The MicroVAX 3900 was an S-box rackmounted in a 19 inch cabinet...
  One usually found larger (RA9x) class disks mounted in the space
  above the S-box.  The MicroVAX 3800 was similar to the MicroVAX 3900,
  but the S-box was mounted as a standalone pedestal, and not in the 
  19 inch rack cabinetry...

  MicroVAX 3800 (pedestal, pedestal only) is about 27 inches tall...
  MicroVAX 3900 (rackmount, rack and all) is about 42 inches tall...

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




