Subject: Announcing C-Kermit 7.0
Date: 2000/01/06
Message-ID: <8539ft$c50$1@newsmaster.cc.columbia.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 569313703
X-Trace: newsmaster.cc.columbia.edu 947202365 12448 128.59.39.2 (6 Jan 2000 23:46:05 GMT)
Organization: Columbia University
NNTP-Posting-Date: 6 Jan 2000 23:46:05 GMT
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,alt.sys.pdp11
X-Complaints-To: postmaster@columbia.edu


After 3+ years of development, C-Kermit 7.0 is released, replacing
version 6.0 from 1996.  You can find the new release at:

  http://www.columbia.edu/kermit/ckermit.html

The web page lists and explains all the new features and contains
download links.

Not counting VMS (the VMS version will be announced separately on the
VMS newsgroup), C-Kermit 7.0 has been built on the following ex-DEC
ex-platforms (or non-ex-DEC OS's running on ex-DEC hardware):

 . DECstation 5000 with Ultrix 4.3 and 4.4
 . Alpha with Digital UNIX 3.2
 . Alpha with Compaq Tru64 Unix 4.0E, F, and G
 . Alpha with NetBSD 1.4

This is obviously a rather short list.  Many binaries are missing and
I'd like to add them to the archive before it's too late (i.e. before the
next-to-last example of some platform is switched off and the last one
has no way to build Kermit, but needs it urgently).  A few examples that
spring to mind are:

 . VAX with 4.1, 4.2, or 4.3 BSD
 . VAX with Ultrix, any version
 . VAX with NetBSD
 . VAX with AT&T System VR3
 . VAX with Bell Research UNIX V10
 . Ultrix versions other than 4.3 and 4.4, on anything
 . PC versions of OSF/1
 . Alpha versions of OSF/1 prior to 3.1
 . DECstation with OSF/1 1.x
 . DECstation with 4.4BSD ("Sprite")
 . DECstation with Mach 2.6
 . DECstation with NetBSD
 . What did I forget?

PDP-11s are pretty much ruled out for C-Kermit 7.0; it's just too big.  I
was actually able to build it, after much shuffling of overlays, on 2.11
BSD, but the result is useless -- all the features had to be stripped out
and there's too much static storage or something, which makes it go really
slow.

For such systems -- well, for any system really -- there is also a new,
stripped-down, send-and-receive-only Kermit program for UNIX called
G-Kermit (also written in C):

  http://www.columbia.edu/kermit/gkermit.html

and this one builds quite nicely on 2.11 BSD, and gives rather snappy file
transfers too.  It should be extremely portable -- that's one of the main
ideas behind it, since it deliberately steers clear of platform specifics
beyond the bare minimum (BSD vs SYSV vs POSIX).  It should be buildable on
Ultrix-11, 2.4BSD on the -11, Venix on the Pro-3xx, and who knows, maybe
even Original UNIX on the PDP-7 (who has one?)

If you can make any C-Kermit 7.0 or G-Kermit 1.00 binaries that are not
listed in this notice (or, more up to date, the binary lists in the web
pages, which are updated daily), please let me know and we'll add them to
the archive, and then some day somebody somewhere will be very grateful.

Thanks!

- Frank



