If the first disklabel -z fails you can just go on with a disklabel -wr
and put some filesystems on it. You don't have to do a disklabel -z
anymore. The first disklabel -z is just to be sure there aren't any
disklabel on the disk.

The primary reason why I stated it was important no to re-use an
existing and unknown disklabel is that I've seen a SAP production system
go down because someone re-used the existing disklabel when they added
new storage. Those disklabels actually came from a time where the disks
were stripeset members on a HSZ70.
At first this worked, but when SAP tried to write to an area on the disk
that didn't exist because the disk was (alot) smaller than the disklabel
stated... AdvFS panic, and lots of them !!!


