How Important is Reliability in a Total Cost of Ownership
Analysis of Clusters?

A Detailed Analysis of the Total Cost of Ownership of Four
Different RISC-Based Cluster Solutions and the Impact of
Reliability on the Total Cost of Ownership

TechWise Research, Inc.

November 1999


Abstract

The installed base of server clusters has been growing
at a rapid pace for several years.  These systems provide
customers with the performance and reliability necessary for
mission critical applications.  Clusters often serve as the heart
of an organization's computing environment.  Despite this
strategic role, few detailed studies have been conducted
regarding the Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) of these systems.
This white paper provides a robust analysis of the three-year TCO
for four different mid-range RISC-based clusters.  Mid-range
server clusters from Compaq, Hewlett-Packard, IBM, and Sun
Microsystems were compared in terms of their acquisition,
installation, training, configuration, management, and
maintenance costs. In addition to these costs, this paper also
compares the reliability of these four cluster systems.

Results indicate major differences between the four manufacturers
in the area of TCO.  Compaq AlphaServers running OpenVMS have the
lowest overall TCO ($612,000), followed closely by IBM at
$653,000.  Sun's clusters have the third highest TCO at $728,000,
followed by HP at $923,000.  In order to incorporate differences
in reliability between brands in the analysis, TechWise Research
developed a metric called the Reliability-Adjusted TCO(TM)*.
This metric is calculated in two steps.  First, the average
number of unplanned downtime incidences per brand over a
twelve-month period was measured.  Then, using a conservative
estimate for the cost of each downtime incident, annual downtime
costs for each cluster brand were calculated.  After applying
this metric, a more pronounced difference between Compaq and the
other three brands emerged: Compaq AlphaServers running OpenVMS
Clusters have the lowest Reliability-Adjusted TCOTM.
Specifically, Compaq AlphaServers running OpenVMS are less costly
than IBM, Sun, and HP over a three-year period by $281,000,
$311,000, and $371,000, respectively.

This white paper explains and summarizes the results of the
research study.

Background on Total Cost of Ownership

One of the primary methods for deploying RISC-based servers is
in a server farm or cluster. These clusters have two or more
servers linked together to provide automated failover and/or
parallel processing capabilities.  Each of the major RISC server
manufacturers offers its own cluster software solution.  The
features and functionality of this software, along with the
server platforms themselves, determine many of the cluster's
performance characteristics.  One aspect of server clusters
that has not yet been extensively studied is Total Cost of
Ownership (TCO).  The purpose of this paper is to provide one
of the first in-depth analyses of the total cost of ownership
of several different brands of RISC-based server clusters.
Although TCO is not the only factor in the purchase decision,
this information should help IT Managers evaluate solutions
from different hardware manufacturers.

The overall concept of Total Cost of Ownership continues to
receive a tremendous amount of attention within the IT community.
Customers realize that the initial purchase price of a system
represents only one part of its true cost.  Server clusters, due
to their very nature, raise the importance of TCO to a new level.
 Previous reports have shown that the TCO of different brands of
RISC-based servers can vary by 40 percent or more1.  This
difference in absolute dollars for clusters may be magnified by
the fact that server clusters are comprised of at least two, and
often more than four, servers.  In addition to this hardware
"multiplier effect," the actual clustering software can have a
major impact on TCO.  This software influences both the cluster's
start-up and management costs.  It also can impact the overall
reliability of the cluster.  Since clusters are often installed
in mission critical areas, actual availability (i.e., up-time)
will also affect the total cost of ownership.

The idea that other factors besides purchase price contribute to
a server's (and hence a cluster's) true cost of ownership is
widely accepted.  As a result, several independent studies have
been published in recent years that compare the TCO of various
server brands.  However, there is no standard formula or practice
when it comes to measuring a server's TCO.  Every company has a
slightly different approach.  Some, for instance, consider only
the direct out-of-pocket expenses of the system price and service
contract. Other approaches include some hidden costs such as
training, on-going management and maintenance.  These costs can
have a major impact on a cluster's true TCO.


1 Are Proprietary RISC Servers Really More Expensive than Their
  UNIX Alternatives? (c) TechWise Research, May 1999


Regardless of technique, many Fortune 1000 firms use TCO analyses
as part of their overall purchase process.  However, few, if any,
reports measure the TCO for RISC-based clusters based on newly
introduced systems.  This white paper is designed to address this
need and focuses on the following four types of RISC clusters:

o Compaq AlphaServers running OpenVMS and OpenVMS Clusters
  software

o HP 9000 servers running HP-UX and MC/Service Guard
  software

o IBM RS/6000 servers running AIX and HACMP ES software

o Sun SPARC servers running Solaris and Sun Cluster
  software

Components of the Total Cost of Ownership Model TechWise
Research's TCO model includes the following four main components:

o Acquisition Cost - the list price of the cluster and all
  related hardware/system software needed to bring the cluster to
  the baseline configuration. (The cost of application software is
  not included.)

o Start-Up Cost - the cost to install and configure the cluster,
  including both internal and external labor costs, as well as the
  cost to train staff on how to manage the cluster.

o On-Going Operational Costs - the costs to manage the cluster on a
  daily basis as well as to maintain the cluster. Maintenance also
  includes the internal costs associated with solving any problems
  that arise with the cluster.

o Service Agreements - the actual external cost each manufacturer
  charges for their annual service agreement.  Given the mission
  critical application for most clusters, we used 7 x 24 x 365
  service for the analysis.


Two-Phase Methodology

o Phase One

  - Quantitative telephone survey of qualified IT professionals to
    collect the operational costs of the server clusters.

    o Interviewed 125 IT Managers in the U.S..

    o Operational costs include costs for training, installation,
      management, and customer maintenance.

    o Collected profiling information to better understand cluster
      use and ensure accurate representation of the market.

o Phase Two

  - List price and three-year service agreement costs were
    collected from IDEAS International on September 16, 1999.


Two-Phase Methodology Maximizes Value of the Model

TechWise Research utilized a two-phase approach to quantify the
TCO for current RISC-based server cluster offerings from four
leading manufacturers. In Phase One, we completed 125 quantitative
surveys with IT professionals to measure the operational costs
associated with server clusters that have been in production for
at least two months. In Phase Two, current acquisition and
service agreement costs, as of September 16, 1999, were collected
from IDEAS International.  IDEAS International is recognized
worldwide as a leading authority on systems technology,
specializing in the research of comparative information on
computer systems, products, and associated technologies. Their
current system and service pricing for most leading server
manufacturers is updated daily with new product and price
announcements. Two customers can pay very different prices for
two identical systems depending on when they buy them and what
level of discount they can negotiate from their channel.  By
using current list prices from IDEAS International, we eliminated
any time and purchasing power bias.  These findings, from both
Phase 1 and Phase 2, were then combined to calculate the overall
TCO.


Server Mokels Included in the Analysis

Cluster Brand            Server Models

Compaq AlphaServer       4100,2100,4000,3600,1000A OpenVMS

HP 9000                  K400,K200,K100,D200,D300

IBM RS/6000              300 series, C series, F series,
                         PowerServer. SP2

Sun SPARC                UE2,UE450, UE4000, UE4500, UE5000,
                         UE3000


In a previous study, TechWise Research conducted a qualitative
study to validate this new approach of measuring TCO. In that
study we learned that the operating system software is the
primary driver of on-going operational costs.  As long as the
operating system has not gone through a major revision, updating
a server with a faster processor or motherboard will have little
to no impact on on-going operational costs. The primary benefit
of our approach is that it allows us to quantify the TCO for
clusters based on servers shortly after their introduction.
Traditional approaches would require that we wait several years
for clusters based on these systems to be installed and running
before collecting our data.  Our approach means customers can
evaluate the TCO of new server cluster offerings when it's most
valuable to do so, right after introduction.  The quantitative
surveys for this study were all conducted in the United States.
To participate, respondents had to be familiar with the
installation, management, and maintenance costs of a qualifying
cluster.  The chart above lists the top server models, by
manufacturer, that were in the clusters included in our analysis.
 The models are listed in order of use; the models respondents
use most often are listed first.


Accounting for System Variation

Cluster Configurations Used In The Analysis Phase Two

o The following cluster configuration was used for the study:

  - Number of Servers              3
  - Number of Processors             12 (4 per server)
  - Total Memory              3 GB (1 GB per server)
  - Total Internal Physical Storage  27 GB (9 GB per server)
  - Storage Array              91 GB

o The following mid-range server models were selected for the
  analysis because they represent comparable machines:

  - Compaq AlphaServer OpenVMS:      ES40 Model 2
  - HP 9000:                         N4000
  - IBM RS/6000:                     H70
  - Sun SPARC:                       UE3500

o System and service pricing was gathered for these cluster
  configurations from IDEAS International.

In order to ensure comparability, the models included in the
final TCO analysis were selected based on their relatively
recent introduction, similar performance levels, and
expandability.  Additionally, each cluster was configured with
the identical quantities of servers, processors, memory, and
storage. The base configurations used, as well as the actual
models included in the analysis, are detailed to the left.


Who Was Surveyed

The chart on the right illustrates the industry breakdown of
the 125 study participants.  The study's sample is based on a
broad mix of industries. However, Government and Education were
limited to 20 percent of the overall sample. Thismwas to ensure
that the unique characteristics of these installations did not
bias the overall findings.  All interviews were conducted
randomly by industry.

Most respondents work for large companies with annual revenues in
excess of $100 million.  The majority also have more than one
cluster at their site; the average is five.  Respondents were
asked to provide information for the cluster with which they were
most familiar.  Nearly two out of three clusters included in this
study have been installed for more than one year, which
demonstrates that respondents have had more than adequate
experience with their clusters' on-going operational costs.

Over half (58%) of the clusters are used for database
applications.  Other common uses are online transaction
processing, e-mail, and process manufacturing.  The top
commercial applications run on the clusters are Oracle (47%),
Sybase (13%), PeopleSoft (8%), Informix (7%), SAP (7%), and Lotus
Notes (5%). Nearly half of the respondents indicated that TCO was
important in their cluster purchase decision. Furthermore, 54
percent indicate that TCO will be more important in future
purchase decisions.


Total Cost of Ownership Findings

Compaq OpenVMS clusters have the overall lowest three-year TCO
at $612,000, followed closely by IBM at $653,000.  Sun's cluster
has the third highest TCO at $728,000, followed by HP at $923,000.
There is a 50 percent difference between the TCO of Compaq OpenVMS
clusters and HP UNIX clusters. Some of that difference has to do
with the acquisition price for the two different clusters.

The table below lists the four component costs (acquisition,
start-up, on-going operational, and service agreements) for all
four cluster brands.  It shows that IBM has the lowest list price
(i.e., acquisition) for its cluster at $290,000, while HP's list
price is the highest at $578,000.  However, 64 percent of the
cost difference between HP and IBM is due to the higher price HP
charges for its processors.  If we had chosen a smaller cluster
configuration with fewer processors, HP would be more price
competitive.

Relative to the other three components, start-up costs represent
a small share of the TCO.  Compaq and Sun have the lowest costs
in this area.  However, on-going operational costs vary widely
among the different brands.  One reason for this difference is
the number of staff hours spent managing and maintaining the
cluster.  Another factor is the performance and capabilities of
the clustering software itself.  Compaq and Sun have virtually
identical on-going operational costs with HP running a close
third.  IBM's clusters are the most expensive in this regard.
Finally, we list the current price each manufacturer charges for
three years of 7x24x365 support.  IBM and Compaq charge roughly
half of what HP and Sun charge for this service.

The Impact of  Reliability on TCO

Most respondents indicated that one of the key functions of their
cluster is to provide automated failover capabilities.  These
clusters are truly being used for mission critical applications
that must run 24 hours a day, 365 days a year.  Any unplanned
downtime caused by the cluster crashing would have significant
associated costs.  Separate studies from independent firms,
such as DataQuest, show that the cost of downtime on an hourly
basis averages $100,000 with some industries exceeding $1 million.
As part of this TCO study, we collected information regarding
how often each cluster crashed during the past 12 months.  Many
respondents indicated their cluster did not crash at all in the
past year.  The following table summarizes the average number of
incidences of unplanned downtime over a twelve-month period for
each brand:

Number of Unplanned Downtime Incidences per Year

Average Incidences Per Year     Compaq OpenVMS      1.2

Average Incidences Per Year     IBM RS/6000         2.8

Average Incidences Per Year     Sun SPARC           2.5

Average Incidences Per Year     HP 9000             1.6


Although not specifically measured, we can estimate the effect
this finding would have on each cluster's TCO.  A conservative
assumption would be that each time a cluster crashed, it would
result in a $50,000 downtime cost per incident, regardless of
brand.  Over a three-year period, factoring in these downtime
costs would add $180,000, $420,000, $375,000, and $240,000
respectively to the TCO of Compaq, IBM, Sun, and HP clusters.
Several key conclusions result from analyzing these
Reliability-Adjusted TCOTM figures. First, the cost of downtime
can be a significant part of the overall cluster TCO.  Second,
adding downtime costs changes the overall study findings.  The
chart above shows the Reliability-Adjusted TCO(TM) findings.  Two
changes have occurred.  First, Compaq's clusters now have an even
greater TCO advantage.  Second, HP's clusters are now more cost
competitive.  It is likely that with a smaller configuration,
HP's clusters would have a lower TCO than several of the other
brands.  This is because HP's better-than-average availability
would overcome HP's higher-than-average processor pricing.


Conclusions

Total Cost of Ownership is an important factor when purchasing
RISC clusters. This study shows that for robust clusters built
around current models of department-level servers, Compaq
AlphaServers running OpenVMS Clusters have the lowest overall
TCO. Compaq AlphaServers running OpenVMS are $41,000, $116,000,
and $311,000 less costly than IBM, Sun, and HP, respectively,
over a three-year period.

Traditional methods for measuring the TCO of clustered servers
may be misleading because they ignore the cost of cluster
downtime.  When these costs are factored into the TCO equation,
the actual acquisition cost represents less than half the
cluster's TCO over a three-year period. Both Compaq and HP
clusters provide better than average reliability.  Depending on
each customer's unique situation and their associated cost of
downtime, this finding may be more important than all the
remaining TCO components combined.  When the average costs of
cluster downtime are taken into consideration, Compaq
AlphaServers running OpenVMS Clusters have the lowest
Reliability-Adjusted TCO(TM). Specifically, Compaq AlphaServers
running OpenVMS are $281,000, $311,000, and $371,000 less costly
than IBM, Sun, and HP, respectively, over a three- year period.

In order to maximize return on investment, managers should factor
both TCO and reliability into their cluster purchase decisions.
Of course, other factors such as software features, application
availability, and quality of service and support offered also
contribute to the overall value of a cluster and should not be
ignored.

