In article <84vbos$v43$1@inet-1.pnu.com>,
	Pete <pete@no-spam.net> writes:
> I wonder why I get
> Floating point exception (core dumped)
> then I run this very simple C code?
> 
...
>   for (i=0;i<1000000000;i++)
>     x=x*x;
...

You get a floating point exception because the result of your computation
is to big to be represented as a floating point number (3.14^2^100000000).
If you want to do floating point coumputations with infinities you have to
compile your program with the option -ieee. Your result will then be a
positive infinity.
