In article <850bot$ir1$1@supernews.com>, "Frank Mattes" <mattes@rfhsm.ac.uk> writes:
:...connect a pc as a console to a microvax 3400...
:I was getting a boot prompt...
:After typing BOOT, the microvax sems to boot up with vms 5.5.

  comp.os.vms is usually a better spot for OpenVMS (VMS) questions...
  (comp.sys.dec covers a far wider variety of topics...)

:However, the boot process stopped with a $ prompt. No login screen.

  Whatever system root you have booted into is a standalone BACKUP 
  root, not an OpenVMS VAX system root.  You *might* have an OpenVMS 
  root elsewhere on this disk, but (while possible) that is rather 
  unlikely...

  Standalone BACKUP is used to install OpenVMS VAX, and to make and
  restore backups of the OpenVMS VAX system disk...

:Can anybody point me to a page where I can find out what happend ?
:Is this normal ?

  Yep, if you want to boot standalone BACKUP...

  At the console >>> prompt, try SHOW DEVICE or SHOW CONFIG and see 
  what other disks (if any) are on the MicroVAX 3400 system, and try 
  booting from those...  Console boot syntax is "BOOT DIAn".  

  If you find an OpenVMS root, please see the OpenVMS FAQ for details 
  on determining the system password...  The FAQ is available via:

    http://www.openvms.digital.com/

:%BACKUP-I-IDENT, Stand-alone BACKUP V5.5-2; the date is  5-JAN-2000
:00:02:14.31
:$

  You will want to acquire the DECUS hobbyist licenses and media kit,
  and install OpenVMS VAX onto the box...  A clean installation is
  normally much easier to deal with...  The OpenVMS FAQ has details
  on the hobbyist program, too...

