This is from the linux alpha discussion group.
this may help
> The Alpha PCI ATX motherboards don't do power switching the same way
> standard Intel motherboards do. The standard ATX power supply looks for a
> ground on pin 14 of the connector to power on. This is the only green wire
> on the connector. Jumpering it to one of the black (ground) wires will turn
> the power supply on all the time. Most ATX power supplies have a physical
> switch on the back that can be used to control power to the machine.
>
> I solved the problem by tack-soldering a jumper on the power connector on
> the motherboard between pins 13 and 14. You can tell which these are by
> plugging in the connector and seeing where the green wire goes.

Hi,
If  you're going to solder on the pins as mentioned above, maybe
consider
soldering on something that can act as posts for the ATX soft power
switch
connector to plug onto. I did, and changed the case power switch from a
momentary switch to an on-off switch. That way, the ATW main power
supply switch
can stay on, and I can turn the sucker on from the switch on the front
of the
box...  Just be careful soldering on the motherboard... some soldering
irons can
generate enough EMF or static (not sure..) to blow chips.

Just for info, the PC164 is likely the most flexible of the bunch. It
can run
Linux, NT, OpenVMS, and Tru64. (The SX and LX can't run OpenVMS.) It may
be
'only' the PC164, but its fast and flexible...and isn't that where the
fun
starts?..

Evan



