Terry Murphy wrote:

> What is the source of this statistic? Intel has about 85% of the PC
> microprocessor market, so perhaps about 80% of the total microprocessor
> market. I understand that AMD, Motorola, and IBM also use VMS in their
> fabs (can anyone confirm/deny this?) Sun, Compaq, and HP use one of the
> previously mentioned companies for fabs. I'm not sure about MIPS, IDT,
> and Cyrix, but it seems they cannot be a large share. So it seems that
> the statistic should either be 80% (just counting Intel) or something
> closer to 100% (counting everybody).
>
> -- Terry

I think the 80% figure is for the total semiconductor industry, not just MPU
manufactures.

While I can't confirm the exact percent I do know that the two most popular MES
systems used by semiconductor manufactures are Workstream and Promis, I believe
Workstream is by far the most popular.  Both of these systems run on VMS and use
very old technology.  For example Workstream has a DBMS database and Promis uses
RMS index files to store data.  The reason companies haven't moved away from
these two products is a combination of the cost of moving and the lack of a
proven replacement.  Promis now has an NT version, but I'm not sure any major
FAB is using it.  Consilium, who sells Workstream, has produced some Unix (HP I
think) MES systems, but they were not adopted by many.  The are now working on a
next generation product called FAB300 (for 300mm FABs) I believe it will be NT
based. There is also a company called FASTech that makes a product called
Factoryworks which uses both Unix and NT.  I think IBM uses an internally
designed MES system.

As for Compaq using this in it's marketing, it wouldn't work all that well. Sun
and HP systems are also used in most FAB's.  Even Digital's fabs used HP and Sun
machines.

-Bruce






