"Bill Todd" <billtodd@foo.mv.com> writes:

> Introducing a new 32-bit-size variable-length record type in RMS over time,
> however, would have been reasonable (given some real indication of need).

Fortran unformatted I/O, as mentioned. BACKUP with saveset records larger than
32K - already possible on tape (up to 64K), which breaks COPY to disk, and one
would like to have a native method of handling large records for streaming
devices directly (e.g., DLTs).

> But, as I noted above, rolling your own using block I/O has always been an
> option as well (at least for sequential-file-like accessing), and isn't
> really very difficult.

Direct QIO isn't really an option: for one, many other applications, most of
them parts of VMS, would break, which decreases the attrativeness of the
approach; for another, the good thing about RMS is that all programs get its
benefits, not just the few you have control over (think record locking et al).
In the process, back pointers (see the usual way unformatted I/O is done for
Fortran on Unix systems) could have been added.

	Jan



