Georg Schwarz wrote:

> I think it's not a SCSI floppy in the VAXstation 3100. =

Hmm? One moment. DUA2 points to a MSCP drive... ???
Ohh, my mistake. It seems to be a floppy connected to the SCSI/MFM
controller found in the early VS3100 models. According to
/amd/cd0/vax/INSTALL.txt this floppy is not supported in the GENERIC
Kernel, bootloader, ... So I think there is no way to install NetBSD via
this floppy. Sorry.

> >NetBSD on a VAX is via netboot. See the excelent NetBSD Netboot HOWTO =
at
> This would mean much more work to me.
It is not that much more work. A litle bit of mop, bootp, nfs and it
will work. =

1. MOP
Get, compile and start mopd. (See netboot HOWTO)
Place the bootstrap in to /tftpboot/mop
---
[jkunz@MrWinterbottom jkunz]$ ls -l /tftpboot/mop
total 286
lrwxr-xr-x  1 root  wheel      8 Jan  2 12:16 08002b036e6e.SYS ->
boot.mop
-rw-r--r--  1 root  wheel  74240 Dec 31 23:07 boot.mop
---

2. bootp
---
[jkunz@MrWinterbottom jkunz]$ tail -7 /etc/bootptab
james:\
        ht=3Dethernet:\
        ha=3D08002b036e6e\
        ip=3D192.168.1.7:\
        bf=3D/tftpboot/mop/netbsd:\
        rp=3D/usr/vax/james
---
Delete the "#" in front of the "bootps" line in your /etc/inetd.conf,
and restart inetd.

3. nfs
Add a line like this to your /etc/exports.
/usr/vax/james      james(rw,no_root_squash)
The kernel-nfsd / mountd found in RedHat 6.[01] does not interoperate
with NetBSD due to incompatibilities. =


4. set up the nfs-root
Extract base.tgz and etc.tgz in /usr/vax/james. Do a "./MAKEDEV all" in
/usr/vax/james/dev. Copy the kernel to /usr/vax/james/netbsd
-- =




